WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.048
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.348 --> 00:00:09.211
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.511 --> 00:00:15.925
Unit four: Advanced Academic Research. Today you will condense arguments with nominalized abstraction.

4
00:00:16.225 --> 00:00:18.877
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:23.287
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.557
Meta-analysis.

7
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.953
Meta-analysis means a statistical synthesis of results from many studies.

8
00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.931
A meta-analysis of forty trials confirmed the drug's effect.

9
00:00:35.517 --> 00:00:38.285
Your turn. Say: Meta-analysis.

10
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:42.576
Meta-analysis.

11
00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:44.887
Longitudinal study.

12
00:00:45.883 --> 00:00:50.623
Longitudinal study means research that observes the same subjects over a long period.

13
00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:54.575
A longitudinal study followed the children for twenty years.

14
00:00:55.183 --> 00:00:58.045
Your turn. Say: Longitudinal study.

15
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.598
Longitudinal study.

16
00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.164
Statistical significance.

17
00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:10.715
Statistical significance means the likelihood that a result is not due to chance.

18
00:01:11.317 --> 00:01:14.036
The difference lacked statistical significance.

19
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:18.429
Your turn. Say: Statistical significance.

20
00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:23.266
Statistical significance.

21
00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:25.320
Literature review.

22
00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:30.348
Literature review means a critical survey of existing research on a topic.

23
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:34.366
Her literature review exposed a gap in Cambodian data.

24
00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.533
Your turn. Say: Literature review.

25
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:41.855
Literature review.

26
00:01:42.617 --> 00:01:44.094
Theoretical framework.

27
00:01:45.083 --> 00:01:48.673
Theoretical framework means the set of theories that structures a study.

28
00:01:49.283 --> 00:01:52.490
The theoretical framework drew on human capital theory.

29
00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:56.188
Your turn. Say: Theoretical framework.

30
00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:00.522
Theoretical framework.

31
00:02:01.283 --> 00:02:02.621
Reproducibility.

32
00:02:03.617 --> 00:02:08.426
Reproducibility means the ability of other researchers to obtain the same results.

33
00:02:09.017 --> 00:02:11.957
The reproducibility crisis shook psychology.

34
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.527
Your turn. Say: Reproducibility.

35
00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:19.855
Reproducibility.

36
00:02:20.617 --> 00:02:21.966
Epistemic rigor.

37
00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:26.935
Epistemic rigor means strict standards for justifying knowledge claims.

38
00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:31.349
Epistemic rigor demands that every claim be traceable to evidence.

39
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:35.276
Your turn. Say: Epistemic rigor.

40
00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:39.610
Epistemic rigor.

41
00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:41.525
Peer critique.

42
00:02:42.517 --> 00:02:46.409
Peer critique means evaluation of work by other experts in the field.

43
00:02:47.017 --> 00:02:49.933
Peer critique exposed a flaw in the sampling method.

44
00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:53.760
Your turn. Say: Peer critique.

45
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:57.787
Peer critique.

46
00:02:58.550 --> 00:02:59.795
Meta-cognition.

47
00:03:00.783 --> 00:03:04.443
Meta-cognition means awareness and regulation of one's own thinking.

48
00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:08.408
Meta-cognition helps researchers notice their own biases.

49
00:03:09.017 --> 00:03:12.041
Your turn. Say: Meta-cognition.

50
00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.249
Meta-cognition.

51
00:03:16.983 --> 00:03:18.182
Triangulation.

52
00:03:19.183 --> 00:03:23.609
Triangulation means using several methods or sources to confirm a finding.

53
00:03:24.217 --> 00:03:27.505
Triangulation combined surveys, interviews and test scores.

54
00:03:28.117 --> 00:03:30.665
Your turn. Say: Triangulation.

55
00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:34.849
Triangulation.

56
00:03:35.633 --> 00:03:39.540
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:46.728
Visal, a study claims that students who drink coffee get better grades. Should schools serve coffee?

58
00:03:47.817 --> 00:03:55.464
Not yet. The reliability of that claim depends on its design. Correlation alone offers no proof of causation.

59
00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.120
But the sample was huge.

60
00:03:59.217 --> 00:04:08.963
Size isn't everything. The absence of a control group and the reliance on self-reported data weaken the conclusion considerably.

61
00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.434
What would convince you?

62
00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:22.308
The replication of the result in independent studies, ideally a meta-analysis. The reproducibility of findings is what determines their reliability.

63
00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:25.130
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:25.983 --> 00:04:29.306
Grammar focus: Nominalized abstraction.

65
00:04:29.606 --> 00:04:30.758
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:34.843
The reproducibility of findings determines their reliability.

67
00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:38.702
The absence of a control group weakens the conclusion.

68
00:04:39.617 --> 00:04:42.638
The replication of results strengthens public confidence.

69
00:04:43.600 --> 00:05:00.094
Academic English turns verbs and adjectives into nouns (reproduce → reproducibility; absent → absence) to pack ideas densely. Compare: 'If others can reproduce findings, they are reliable' → 'The reproducibility of findings determines their reliability.'

70
00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:04.814
Be careful! Don't say: The reproduce of findings matters.

71
00:05:06.767 --> 00:05:09.869
Say: The reproducibility of findings matters.

72
00:05:10.169 --> 00:05:11.542
Use the noun form.

73
00:05:12.333 --> 00:05:14.777
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:15.567 --> 00:05:17.322
The Replication Crisis.

75
00:05:17.622 --> 00:05:26.240
During the 2010s, the failure of many landmark psychology experiments to replicate triggered what became known as the reproducibility crisis.

76
00:05:27.233 --> 00:05:34.295
The causes included small samples, selective reporting of significant results and pressure on researchers to publish novel findings.

77
00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:43.778
In response, the adoption of pre-registration, which requires the publication of hypotheses before data collection, has spread across disciplines.

78
00:05:44.767 --> 00:05:53.848
Meta-analysis and triangulation provide further safeguards, since the convergence of independent evidence is harder to manufacture than a single striking result.

79
00:05:54.833 --> 00:06:02.766
Ultimately, the trustworthiness of research depends less on any individual study than on the epistemic rigor of the community that scrutinises it.

80
00:06:03.867 --> 00:06:07.462
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:06:08.317 --> 00:06:10.467
The blank of the data weakened the study.

82
00:06:10.767 --> 00:06:15.837
A, unreliable. B, unreliability. C, unreliably.

83
00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.504
The answer is B: unreliability.

84
00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:26.310
A statistical synthesis of results from many studies is a blank.

85
00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:31.285
A, meta-analysis. B, literature review. C, peer critique.

86
00:06:34.833 --> 00:06:37.274
The answer is A: meta-analysis.

87
00:06:38.417 --> 00:06:41.705
Using several methods to confirm a finding is blank.

88
00:06:42.005 --> 00:06:47.957
A, meta-cognition. B, statistical significance. C, triangulation.

89
00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.115
The answer is C: triangulation.

90
00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:57.424
Choose the most academic sentence.

91
00:06:57.724 --> 00:07:05.499
A, Because they didn't check, it was wrong.. B, The lack of verification undermined the findings.. C, They didn't check so it's bad..

92
00:07:09.033 --> 00:07:13.320
The answer is B: The lack of verification undermined the findings..

93
00:07:14.483 --> 00:07:17.632
According to the reading, pre-registration requires…

94
00:07:17.932 --> 00:07:24.035
A, publishing hypotheses before data collection. B, larger budgets. C, avoiding peer review.

95
00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:31.387
The answer is A: publishing hypotheses before data collection.

96
00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:35.589
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:38.879
What makes academic research trustworthy?

98
00:07:55.683 --> 00:07:57.218
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:57.518 --> 00:08:34.498
The trustworthiness of research depends primarily on the transparency of its methods and the reproducibility of its results. A single impressive study, however large, can be distorted by chance, bias or flawed design. Confidence grows when independent teams obtain the same findings using different methods, which is the purpose of triangulation and meta-analysis. Peer critique also exposes weaknesses authors overlook. Ultimately, rigor is a property of the scientific community, not of one paper.

100
00:08:34.798 --> 00:08:36.635
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:41.816
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and nominalized abstraction.

102
00:08:42.116 --> 00:08:46.905
Today's value is rigor. Check every step carefully before you trust a conclusion.

103
00:08:47.205 --> 00:08:49.915
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

104
00:08:50.215 --> 00:08:53.274
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

